    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T13:42:07Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Street Lighting in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore the evidence for Iran's street-lighting modernization, the difference between pilots and widespread adoption, and remaining research gaps."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "different-lighting-functions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different lights, different functions",
        "content": "Urban lighting is a broader subject than the lamps lining a road. Iran Omid, an Iranian lighting supplier, describes work involving roadways, parks, squares, recreational areas, and decorative installations, with municipal, public, and private clients. Its account illustrates the range of applications within the domestic lighting industry. It should not be read as an inventory of public assets or an independent assessment of their condition. [s1]\n\nThis distinction helps clarify the subject of street lighting. A roadway installation, a park-lighting scheme, and illumination intended to emphasize a building's appearance may sit within the same commercial sector, but evidence about one does not establish the quality or coverage of the others. The supplier's portfolio description supports this distinction in applications; it does not establish a nationwide division of maintenance responsibilities. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "documented-retrofits",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Evidence of earlier modernization",
        "content": "Iran's energy balance for Solar Hijri year 1393 documents pilot work replacing street-lighting equipment with LED technology in several electricity-distribution areas, including Golestan, Yazd, South Khorasan, and Isfahan city. The report places these activities within an electricity-efficiency program. This provides historical evidence that LED modernization was already being tested through distribution-company projects, rather than evidence of a recent nationwide conversion. [s2]\n\nThe reporting calendar matters. A Solar Hijri reporting year must retain its original label unless its Gregorian boundaries are established. Equally, pilot results cannot be expanded into national savings without knowing the number and type of installations, operating conditions, and measurement method. The inspected material does not provide a comparable annual series from which to reconstruct adoption across Iranian cities. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-upgrade-claims",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How to read upgrade claims",
        "content": "The domestic industry's description of LED products emphasizes alternatives to older metal-halide and high-pressure-sodium light sources. This establishes the technology being offered, while leaving the performance of a particular public installation to be demonstrated separately. A manufacturer's account is useful evidence about its products and stated activities; it cannot establish citywide energy savings or independently verify public-service outcomes. [s1]\n\nFor this page, the strongest defensible conclusion is therefore limited: official historical reporting documents retrofit pilots, and a supplier's account shows the range of urban applications served by the lighting industry. A fuller account needs current utility and municipal records that distinguish installed equipment from proposed replacements, and measured results from expected benefits. The available evidence does not support a claim that modernization is complete or uniform across Iran. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
